{:edb/db-before-t 1201 :edb/db-after-t 1202 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534514 50 #inst "2026-10-12T01:33:44.226Z" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161812 1071 "Given that $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace$, compute $P(X = 18)$." 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161812 1071 "Given that $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace,$ compute $P(X = 18).$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161812 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nand the set\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nhas\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid S &= 21 - 7 + 1 = 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nitems. So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{15},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 7, 8, 9, …, 21 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 18) = f(18) = \\dfrac{1}{15}\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161812 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/bd/bdbe38e4c652e888c4eea890738bccb4d70804cd2b7c23058f72dd2c8996d717.svg)\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace,$ and the set $S = \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 21 \\right\\rbrace$ has $|S|=21 - 7 + 1 = 15$ items. So, we have\n\n![](images/47/4757730008d9d00bdd406065d1aaad881d6b2a95bb65dc9a021982b00cbd9059.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 18) = f(18) = \\dfrac{1}{15}.\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161813 1071 "Given $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16\\right\\rbrace$, compute $P(8 \\leq X < 13)$." 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161813 1071 "Given $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16\\right\\rbrace,$ compute $P(8 \\leq          X < 13).$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161813 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nand the set\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nhas\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid S &= 16 - 7 + 1 = 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nitems. So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 7, 8, 9, …, 16 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nIn $S$, there are $5$ items $(8, 9, 10, 11, 12)$ that satisfy\n\n$$\n8 \\leq X < 13\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(8 \\leq X < 13) = 5 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{10} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161813 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/cf/cf4a1e915e224fdbbbe53116e848c661f93acad19f5637ec09b704a949a4dd05.svg)\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16 \\right\\rbrace,$ and the set $S = \\left\\lbrace 7, 8, 9, \\ldots, 16 \\right\\rbrace$ has $|S|=16 - 7 + 1 = 10$ items. So, we have\n\n![](images/04/048e928b8243c02a895c03b17473898770d6e2738b21bb61a92d86a6e3d52bfc.svg)\n\nIn $S,$ there are $5$ items $(8, 9, 10, 11, 12)$ that satisfy $8 \\leq  X < 13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(8 \\leq  X < 13) = 5 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{10} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161829 1071 "Given $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace$, compute $P(X \\geq 9)$." 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161829 1071 "Given $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace,$ compute $P(X \\geq 9).$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186161829 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nand the set\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace\n$$\n\nhas\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid S &= 14 - 5 + 1 = 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nitems. So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 5, 6, 7, …, 14 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nIn $S$, the items that satisfy\n\n$$\nX \\geq 9\n$$\n\nare $9, 10, 11, \\ldots, 14$. There are\n\n$$\n14 - 9 + 1 = 6\n$$\n\nof these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 9) = 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{10} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186161829 1074 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/a3/a35341e15b714d4d0682b7a254e26cc141f0d4a0f5a5c4b951f91e7da095512d.svg)\n\nHere, we have $X \\sim U \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace,$ and the set $S = \\left\\lbrace 5, 6, 7, \\ldots, 14 \\right\\rbrace$ has $|S|=14 - 5 + 1 = 10$ items. So, we have\n\n![](images/52/526312cdbaa5f0462aafcea89d4719a4a2f058445f367d720b852dbad0048aaa.svg)\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X \\geq 9$ are $9, 10, 11, \\ldots , 14.$ There are $14 - 9 + 1 = 6$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 9) = 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{10} = \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423943 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423947 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423945 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162021 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423946 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423942 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162036 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423944 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186162167 1050 420.0 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186163251 1064 "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **discrete uniform distribution** over a set $S$ if each value in $S$ is equally likely.\n\nThe probability mass function for the discrete uniform distribution on a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S,; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over the set\n\n$$\nS=\\{s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\}\n$$\n\nwe denote it as\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\{s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we say that $X$ is **uniformly distributed** over $S$.\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $T\\sim U\\{1,2,4\\}$. In this case,\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,4\\}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mid S \\mid = 3\n$$\n\nand therefore the probability mass function for $T$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}t ∈ {1, 2, 4}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nA sketch of $f(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbac12f164635cd4efaa94167a4582689d470c3373769e1aa9facc2670f732ef.png)\n\n**Note:** In this lesson, we will make use of the following formula for computing the cardinality of a set of consecutive numbers:\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b \\} \\right| = b - a + 1\n$$\n\nDon't forget about the $+1$ at the end of the formula. The $+1$ is needed because we are including both endpoints $a$ and $b$ in the count. (Not convinced? Notice that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{4, 5 \\} \\right| = 5 - 4 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\n)" 13194139534514 17592186045417 false] [17592186163251 1064 "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **discrete uniform distribution** over a set $S$ if each value in $S$ is equally likely. \n\nThe probability mass function for the discrete uniform distribution on a set $S$ is given by\n\n![](images/61/61fd8cf495b429392260fbad1b671c9c48c8f681827653aaabe73a4dbf6b6ecf.svg)\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over the set $S=\\{ s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\},$ we denote it as\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\{ s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we say that $X$ is **uniformly distributed** over $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $T\\sim U\\{1,2,4\\}.$ In this case, $S = \\{1,2,4\\},$ $|S| = 3,$ and therefore the probability mass function for $T$ is\n\n![](images/d1/d19075d50e24a2fc9d157311535d0665103b1498ef99312606c4cf6c482cfe1a.svg)\n\nA sketch of $f(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbac12f164635cd4efaa94167a4582689d470c3373769e1aa9facc2670f732ef.png)\n\n**Note:** In this lesson, we will make use of the following formula for computing the cardinality of a set of consecutive numbers:\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{ a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b \\} \\right| = b - a + 1\n$$\n\nDon't forget about the $+1$ at the end of the formula. The $+1$ is needed because we are including both endpoints $a$ and $b$ in the count. (Not convinced? Notice that $\\left| \\{ 4, 5 \\} \\right| = 5 - 4 + 1 = 2.$)" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423906 1080 "\\frac{8}{17}" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423906 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423906 1083 #uuid "0a450533-8c93-5f65-b086-433237dedd04" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423907 1080 "\\frac{7}{16}" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423907 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423907 1083 #uuid "16d2517e-9185-5017-857d-9ebe5b8407f6" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423908 1080 "\\frac{1}{9}" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423908 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423908 1083 #uuid "26cab201-dd5a-5522-a7c5-25241811d134" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423909 1080 "\\frac{7}{18}" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423909 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423909 1083 #uuid "35e889ba-9472-5ac8-a25c-ed6d2ba99992" 13194139534514 17592186045417 true] [17592186423